\end{table}

\begin{proposition}
On $K_3$, the canonical divisor is zero, the genus is $1$, the canonical degree is $0$, and the canonical rank is $0$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every vertex has valency $2$, so its canonical coefficient is zero. The graph has three vertices and three edges. The rank statement also follows directly from the theorem on the zero divisor.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
On $K_4$, $K_5$, and $K_6$, respectively, the tuples $(g,K(v),\deg K,r(K))$ are
\[
(3,1,4,2),\qquad(6,2,10,5),\qquad(10,3,18,9).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $n=4,5,6$ into the complete-graph formulas. The rank entries use Riemann--Roch.
\end{proof}

The first three numerical columns are elementary counts. The final column is a consequence of Riemann--Roch. Keeping this distinction explicit avoids circularity.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Closed-form evaluation}
Given $n$, compute
\[
\operatorname{val}=n-1,\quad K(v)=n-3,\quad
 g=\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2,\quad
\deg K=n(n-3),\quad r(K)=g-1.
\]
This uses a constant number of integer operations. A reliable implementation checks $\deg K=2g-2$, which detects the incorrect valency-minus-one convention.

\subsection{Firing on a complete graph}
Represent a divisor by $(d_1,\dots,d_n)$. Firing vertex $i$ once performs
\[
d_i\leftarrow d_i-(n-1),\qquad d_j\leftarrow d_j+1\quad(j\ne i).
\]
The update takes $O(n)$ time and preserves total degree. For a script $f=(f_1,\dots,f_n)$, the Laplacian has the special form
\[
(\Delta f)_i=n f_i-\sum_j f_j.
\]
After computing one sum, the entire update is obtained in $O(n)$ time.

\subsection{Reduced divisors and burning}
Choose a sink $q$. A divisor is $q$-reduced when it is nonnegative away from $q$ and every nonempty set of nonsink vertices contains a vertex whose chip count is smaller than its number of edges leaving that set. Dhar's burning procedure tests this condition: burn the sink, then repeatedly burn any vertex with fewer chips than edges to already burned vertices. If all vertices burn, the divisor is reduced. Otherwise the remaining set supplies a firing step. A direct pass on a dense adjacency matrix costs $O(n^2)$; complete-graph symmetry permits simpler sorted-coordinate bookkeeping.

\subsection{Finite rank testing}
To test $r(D)\geq q$ naively, enumerate every effective divisor $E$ of degree $q$ and decide whether $D-E$ is equivalent to an effective divisor by reduction. The number of candidates is
\[
\binom{n+q-1}{q}.
\]
This is suitable for small examples but grows combinatorially. Parking-function and reduced-divisor methods are preferable for larger instances.

\section{Structural connections}
\subsection{Critical groups and type $A$}
Degree-zero divisors form the lattice
\[
A_{n-1}=\{x\in\mathbb Z^n:\textstyle\sum_i x_i=0\}.
\]
Principal divisors form the Laplacian image, and the quotient is the critical group. For $K_n$, the Laplacian is $nI-J$. On the real zero-sum hyperplane, $J$ vanishes, so the Laplacian acts as multiplication by $n$. The integral quotient is isomorphic to $(\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z)^{n-2}$ and has order $n^{n-2}$. That order is also the number of spanning trees of $K_n$, in agreement with the matrix-tree theorem and Cayley's formula.

\subsection{Parking functions}
After a sink is selected and nonsink coordinates are sorted, reducedness becomes a system of inequalities closely related to parking functions. In a standard zero-based convention, a sorted parking function $(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1})$ satisfies $a_i<i$. The burning algorithm explains the condition and gives finite normal forms for divisor classes. This is a natural route to a complete-graph proof of Riemann--Roch: canonical complementation should reverse the appropriate defect statistic.

\subsection{Permutohedral geometry}
Real divisors modulo constants occupy an $(n-1)$-dimensional quotient. Hyperplanes where coordinates agree divide it into type-$A$ Weyl chambers; sorting chooses one chamber, and firing vectors lie in the type-$A$ root lattice. These chambers form the fan associated with the permutohedron. Since $K_{K_n}$ is a symmetric constant vector, $D\mapsto K-D$ is an affine reflection. The Riemann--Roch correction can therefore be viewed as a prospective integer-point shadow of order-reversing chamber duality.

\subsection{Network interpretations}
Graph Laplacians also govern electrical potentials, diffusion, and consensus. Chip-firing adds integrality and effectiveness constraints. Over the reals, redistribution on the zero-sum hyperplane is straightforward; over the integers, the critical group records a finite arithmetic obstruction. The theory therefore illuminates systems in which local conservative transfers act on indivisible resources.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The complete-graph calculation gives mutually reinforcing checks. Edge counting determines genus. Summing canonical coefficients determines canonical degree. The identity $\deg K=2g-2$ links them. Riemann--Roch then predicts $r(K)=g-1$. A disagreement among these quantities signals either an arithmetic mistake or a normalization mismatch.

A direct parking-function proof of complete-graph Riemann--Roch is a natural next objective. Dhar reduction turns an infinite equivalence problem into finite inequalities on sorted integer vectors; rank should be expressible as a minimum defect, and canonical complementation should reverse that defect.

A second direction is an explicit integral derivation of the critical group via Smith normal form and the type-$A$ root lattice. A third is a direct set of bijections among rooted spanning trees, recurrent chip configurations, and sink-reduced divisor classes, all counted by $n^{n-2}$. Finally, the affine reflection $D\mapsto K-D$ should be studied on the permutohedral fan to seek a geometric explanation of rank duality.

\section{Reproducible numerical protocol}
For each $n\geq1$, count $n-1$ neighbors at a vertex and subtract $2$ for the canonical coefficient. Multiply by $n$ for the degree. Independently count unordered pairs for the edge count and apply $g=|E|-|V|+1$. Check $\deg K=2g-2$. Only then invoke Riemann--Roch to obtain canonical rank. This ordering distinguishes direct computation from a theorem consequence.

Finite checks at $n=3,4,5,6$ illustrate the formulas and expose convention errors, but they do not prove an identity for every $n$. The general numerical formulas follow from symbolic counting; the rank formula follows from the general Riemann--Roch theorem.

\section{Conclusion}
For the complete graph $K_n$, the standard canonical divisor has coefficient $n-3$, not $n-2$. Its genus and degree are
\[
g(K_n)=\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2,
\qquad
\deg(K_{K_n})=n(n-3)=2g(K_n)-2.
\]
The zero divisor has rank zero. Evaluating Riemann--Roch at the canonical divisor therefore gives
\[
r(K_{K_n})=g(K_n)-1=\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2-1.
\]
For $n=3,4,5,6$ the ranks are $0,2,5,9$. The apparent contradictory value disappears once rank is distinguished from $\ell=r+1$ and the canonical divisor is defined by valency minus $2$. Local redistribution, global cycle topology, and canonical duality are thereby seen as facets of one discrete theory.

